welcome {
    Thank you for installing the reqlan semantic engineering toolset.
    These ideas offer an overview of the extension, the reqlan language, and where to learn more.
    
}

one_liner_ideas the simplest way to express an idea in reqlan

block_ideas {
    If you have a more complex idea
    you can use block syntax to express it.
}

references {
    You can reference [one_liner_ideas], [block_ideas], [https://external-links.com] ["./otherfiles.rq"]
}

attributes {
    Block ideas can have attributed with an @ symbol at the start of the line.
    @attribute_with_a_value "a value"
    @attribute_with_a_reference ["./otherfiles.rq"]
    @attribute_without_a_value
    @attribute_forming_a_subidea {
        subidea content
    }
    // some attributes are special: 
    @todo // mark a block as a todo
    @tests ["./tests.ts"] // a link to tests for the requirement
    @implementation ["./file where the requirement implementatinon lives.ts"] 
}


project_resources {
    - Project site: {{SITE_URL}}
    - Documentation and quickstart: {{QUICKSTART_URL}}
    - Source repository: {{GITHUB_URL}}
    - Visual Studio Marketplace: {{VSC_URL}}
    - Open VSX Registry: {{OPENVSX_URL}}
}

extension_overview {
    The extension offers lots of language support and search capabilities.
    The reqlan extension provides language support for `.rq` requirement files in VS Code and compatible editors.
    Use the Reqlan activity bar to inspect neighbourhood context, reference lists, and graph slices for the active requirement.
    In chat, invoke `@reqlan` with `/rq-search` to explore requirements across the workspace.
}

language_overview {
    Requirements are expressed as named ideas with prose, attributes, and cross-references.
    Ideas may use block `{ ... }` syntax or concise one-line declarations.
    Reference related ideas with wiki links and bracket notation, and link to implementation with file references such as `["./auth.ts".login]`.
    Syntax reference and examples are available at {{SITE_URL}}.
}

